  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1141 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nIsentropic lift and the mid-level trough have been producing patchy \nrain and scattered showers throughout the morning as advertised, \nhave even seen a couple of brief thunderstorms. As the mid-level \nreinforcing of the cold air pushes through this afternoon we'll cut \noff that isentropic lift and dry out. This should reach the coast by \naround 6pm this evening. Clouds will clear from west to east setting \nup a good environment for radiational cooling overnight, especially \ninland. Closer to the coast winds will stay a bit elevated to hinder \nthat cooling just a bit. Still expect inland areas to fall into the \n30s and coastal areas into the upper 30s or low 40s. These should be \nthe lowest temperatures since February/March...until tomorrow \nnight. Wind chill values for the inland Coastal Plains could fall \nto around 30 degrees tonight as that wind remains elevated. \n\nWednesday morning looks brisk despite plenty of sunshine, with \ntemperatures in the 40s and 50s with a moderate wind especially in \nthe Coastal Plains. Winds gradually subside in the afternoon and \ninto the evening setting us up again for a cold night. Temperatures \nfall into the 30s nearly area-wide Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning, except for the islands. There remains a low (10-15%) chance \nfor a few locations inland and north to briefly hit freezing, but \nthis will be localized and not last more than an hour or two. \n\nBenign weather will persist through the weekend and into early next \nweek with a gradual warming trend in temps (especially next week) \nand dry conditions. Another cold front may move through mid-next \nweek and cool things down, but confidence isn't strong yet with it \nbeing a fairly new addition to the forecast. If the front doesn't \nmove through we could see highs near 80 by mid-week.",
